## 4. What is real and what is illustrative

| Area | Status |
|---|---|
| Live collection from the Greenhouse and Lever board APIs | Real, on request, through the local API. Verified 9 October: Stripe board 727 postings, Palantir site 313 postings. |
| Saved sample | Real postings from the Stripe board, retrieved 9 October 2026, truncated, labelled as a sample wherever shown. |
| Quotations, counts, intervals, coverage index, briefs, backups | Real and deterministic; covered by automated checks. |
| Curriculum revisions, constraint checks, version history, teaching plans | Real, browser-local. |
| Teaching packs and brief decks (.pptx) | Real and deterministic, generated from saved data with speaker notes; the checks unzip the files and read the slides. |
| The five personas and seeded courses | Prototype identities and example courses. |
| Trends, Relevance Index, Acceptance test, Closed loop | Earlier screens on synthetic market figures and synthetic learner records; labelled illustrative; not evidence of readiness. |
| Optional model streaming for drafting | Exists behind a key; never changes a number; not used in the demo. |

## 5. Verification on 9 October 2026

| Command | Result |
|---|---|
| `npm run typecheck` | Passed. |
| `npm run prove:all` | 152 checks passed across twelve suites: acceptance, backtest, flow, signal, intake, integrity, teaching, curriculum, evidence, collector, research and slides. |
| `npm run build` | Passed with the normal Vite CLI; the main chunk exceeds 500 kB, a warning only. |
| `npm run smoke:curriculum` | Passed: course creation, constraint failure and correction, local adoption, outline export, teaching session and its downloaded teaching pack, assessment review, version history, evidence receipts, rejected misquote, scan, brief sent and its downloaded deck, JSON backup and restore, 390 and 320 pixel reflow, zero page errors. Both decks are unzipped and their slides read. |
| `npm run smoke:workspace` and `npm run smoke:integrity` | Passed. |
| `npm run smoke` | Passed: the full-tour demo adopts a revision, imports sample postings, confirms quotations, maps CLO5, saves a session, generates template items, downloads the teaching pack and the brief deck, sends the brief and has it read, with no page errors. |
| `node scripts/generate-guide.mjs --check` | The generated lecturer guide matches the in-app catalogue. |
| Live API | Greenhouse and Lever fetched; cache, token validation, unknown board, unknown provider and rapid-retry responses behaved as specified. |

Browser checks run in isolated headless profiles with external requests blocked, except the explicit live API check.

## 6. Architecture

A diagram is at `docs/presentation/architecture.png`.

- Browser workspace: React and TypeScript, hash routing, a single persistent store, browser-local persistence with exit flush and visible storage failure, validated JSON backups at schema version 3.
- Deterministic core in `src/lib/`: curriculum constraints and diffs, teaching plans, evidence calculations (citation validator, fingerprints, Wilson intervals, coverage stages, report and brief), board payload normalisation and keyword filters, backup validation.
- Local API: Hono on Node 24. `/api/parse` for xlsx, csv, pdf, pptx and docx. `/api/collect/:provider/:board` for the two permitted job-board hosts, with board-token validation, a one-hour cache, a 15-second gap between fetches of the same board, at most 20 fetches per ten minutes, a 15 MB size cap, a 20-second timeout and plain error messages. `/api/agent` for optional model streaming with a deterministic fallback.
- Planned: a shared API and identity (plan W02) as the contract for Teams through Copilot Studio, Slack and LTI surfaces, so a university reaches Lyceum from the tools it already uses.

## 7. Data, sources and policies

- Source policies are shown in the app for each collection source: owner, method, retention, refresh, policy review date and a note on intended use. Greenhouse and Lever publish these APIs for employers' career pages; Lyceum fetches a named employer's board at low volume on request and does not collect from LinkedIn or anything behind a login. Scheduled or bulk collection would need confirmation of intended use first.
- Provenance per source: lecturer-supplied or collected, with provider, board, posting id, retrieval time and policy label. Publication dates are shown as published and never manufactured from the retrieval time.
- Everything is stored in the lecturer's browser. Backups may contain course material and uploaded learner records and belong to the lecturer. No data is sent to a provider unless a model key is configured, and then only the payload of the specific request.

## 8. Known limitations and not built

- No shared accounts, server permissions, server store or multi-device sync; the browser is the only persistence, with JSON backups as the recovery path.
- Collection covers two documented board APIs and manual paste. No page collectors for employer sites without an API, no snapshots of arbitrary sites, no cross-site vacancy clustering, no scheduled refresh.
- The Lever API does not state the employer name, so the site token is used.
- No comparable time windows yet, so no trend over time is reported.
- Assessment items are template-based; exact mark allocation, model answers and rubrics are pending.
- Slide packs are generated from saved data with no model-written text. Model-drafted slide content with lecturer review, selective regeneration, institutional templates, handouts, and DOCX or PDF export of the brief are pending.
- The Trends, Relevance Index, Acceptance test and Closed loop screens remain on synthetic fixtures and should be quarantined from the lecturer's navigation.
- The optional model integration uses an older SDK and a previous-generation model id; the intake route forces a tool call that current models reject. This is scheduled work.
- Accessibility: labels, focus, keyboard tabs, reflow and contrast are checked by the smokes; a full WCAG 2.2 AA audit with a screen reader is pending.
- No lecturer pilot has run; no educational or commercial validation is claimed.

## 9. Next steps

In order: quarantine the synthetic screens; the provider upgrade; comparable time windows for trends; employer naming for Lever; model-drafted slide content with lecturer review on top of the deterministic packs; DOCX and PDF export of the brief; code splitting; an accessibility pass; an OpenAPI description of collect, report and brief as the contract for the shared API; the shared API and identity when a pilot has a date; then the Teams or Slack surface for the pilot institution's tool.
# Lyceum: travel fellowship pitch pack (9 October 2026, 9 am)

Everything you need for the organizer presentation, in one file: the order of work for the next hours, the five-minute script, the slide content, the live demo path with its proof points, the architecture, the market numbers with sources, the questions you will get, and prompts you can hand to Claude or ChatGPT. Every claim in here is true of the build verified on 9 October 2026; do not let any tool add a claim that is not in this file.

## 1. The next hours, in order

| When | Do | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Now, 15 min | Read Section 2 (the script) once aloud with a timer. | You must own the words before anything else. |
| +15 min, 20 min | Pre-flight the live demo (Section 4): `npm run dev`, open the app, sign in as Dr Sobri, fetch the Stripe board once, run the 90-second path by hand. | Finds any problem while you can still fix it, and warms the cache. |
| +35 min, 10 min | Record the backup video (Section 6): the Play demo end to end, then the 90-second live path. | If the venue network or the laptop fails, you still show the real thing. |
| +45 min, 30 min | Open the deck (Section 3 is its content). Fix wording you would not say, nothing else. | The deck exists; polishing it is the lowest-value use of your time. |
| +75 min, 30 min | Two full rehearsals, timed, deck plus demo. Cut until it fits five minutes. | Timing is what organizers notice. |
| Then | Sleep if you can. Charge the laptop. Put the MP4, the deck and the exported brief on a USB stick. | Nothing is worth presenting tired and without a fallback. |

Do not start new features, new research or new numbers tonight.

## 2. The five-minute script

If you get thirty seconds: "Lyceum is a lecturer's workspace that turns real employer postings into a course decision with receipts. The lecturer runs the research when a decision needs it, confirms every quotation, sees honest counts, and takes a brief to the meeting. It works today, offline, without an AI key. I am asking for travel support to present it and recruit pilot lecturers."

Timings add to 300 seconds including the demo.

**Slide 1, Title, 15 s.** "Good morning. Lyceum is a course workspace for lecturers. In five minutes I will show you a real research run, from a real employer's job board to a brief a lecturer can take to a meeting, and I will tell you exactly what is built and what is not."

**Slide 2, Value: the problem, 35 s.** "A lecturer who wants to refresh a course has three jobs: find out what employers actually ask for, work out what the course already covers, and defend the change to colleagues and the quality committee. Today that evidence lives in browser tabs, inboxes and memory, and the decision is hard to explain later. In Malaysia, DOSM counted 5.98 million graduates in 2024 with unemployment at 3.2 percent but underemployment at 32.2 percent, and MOHE reviews courses for relevance. The lecturer sits between those two facts with no tool built for them."

**Slide 3, What Lyceum does, 45 s.** "Lyceum is not a set of agents that run by themselves. It is what a careful lecturer would do anyway, faster and with receipts. Five steps. Search: fetch postings from a permitted employer board when you ask. Read: name the skills you care about, and confirm each quotation in the posting's exact words; a paraphrase is rejected. See the trend: counts with the denominator in view and a confidence interval, never a score. Decide: map a skill to a course outcome and choose keep, refresh or revise. Brief: a document with every receipt, to download or to send to a colleague inside Lyceum. Nothing runs by itself and nothing changes the course without the lecturer."

**Slide 4, Live demo, 90 s.** Do the Section 4 path. Say only the lines marked there.

**Slide 5, Validate: what is real, 35 s.** "What you saw is the shipped build. 149 automated checks, four headless browser runs and the self-driving demo pass on the current code. The live collectors were verified this week against the Stripe board on Greenhouse, 727 postings, and the Palantir site on Lever, 313 postings. No AI key is needed for any of it. Any saved session also downloads as an editable slide pack, and the brief downloads as slides, generated from the receipts with no AI text. What is not built: page collectors for employer sites that have no API, shared accounts, the Teams and Slack surfaces, and model-drafted slide content. The earlier screens that use synthetic market figures are labelled as illustrative and were not in the demo."

**Slide 6, Verify: how it runs, 25 s.** "The architecture is deliberately small. A browser workspace with a local store and validated JSON backups. A thin local API that only reaches two documented public job-board hosts, with a cache, rate limits and size limits. The core is deterministic: citation validator, duplicate fingerprints, Wilson intervals, coverage stages, the brief. The next step is a shared API and identity, and that is also the door into Teams, Slack and the LMS, so a university does not have to adopt another platform."

**Slide 7, Venture: market and plan, 30 s.** "The numbers are labelled estimates. Malaysia has roughly 45,000 lecturers across about 400 institutions; the world has over 22,000 accredited institutions. Lightcast and Chmura sell alignment to institutions from above, with global datasets and scores. Lyceum sits at the lecturer's desk and inside the tools they already use. The plan is a pilot with three to five lecturers measuring task completion, citation fidelity, time to a brief and repeat use, before any licence."

**Slide 8, The ask, 25 s.** "I am asking for the travel fellowship to present Lyceum at [event], to recruit pilot lecturers there, and to bring back measured results. You have seen a working, honest prototype. The lecturer keeps authority; Lyceum supplies the receipts. Thank you."

## 3. Slide content (paste into any deck tool)

Keep one message per slide, at most four lines of on-screen text, real screenshots from the app, no stock images, no invented charts. Tag each slide with the five V it answers (AWS framework: Value, Visualize, Validate, Verify, Venture).

